.TH DAG 1 "2026-11-13" "KariDNS" "KariDNS Manual"
.\" Generated from docs/dag.md by man/md2man.pl (make man): edit the Markdown manual, not this page.
.SH NAME
dag \- DNS Anomaly Generator, Protocol Inspector, and Multi-Server Verification
Utility
.SH SYNOPSIS
.RS 2
.nf
dag [@server[:port][,...]] [\-p port] [\-b address[#port]] [\-c class] [\-f filename]
    [\-k keyfile] [\-m] [\-q name] [\-t type] [\-u] [\-v] [\-x addr]
    [\-y [hmac:]name:secret] [\-4 | \-6] [name] [type] [class] [queryopt...]
\&
dag [\-h | \-\-help]
\&
dag [\-\-break\-help]
\&
dag \-\-replay <traffic_file> \-\-server1 <host[:port]> [replay\-opts...]
\&
dag [global\-queryopt...] [query...]
.fi
.RE
.SH DESCRIPTION
\fBdag\fR (\fBDNS Anomaly Generator\fR) is a DNS test client, protocol
inspector, multi-server consistency checker, and packet fuzzer developed for the
\fBKariDNS\fR project.
.PP
\fBdag\fR provides dig-compatible command-line syntax and output formatting,
while offering additional testing capabilities:
.IP 1. 4
\fBProtocol Mutation & Fuzzing (\fB\-\-break\fB)\fR: \fBdag\fR can craft
malformed, edge-case, or boundary-testing DNS packets (such as compression
pointer loops, oversized labels, invalid header counts, or TCP stream anomalies)
to evaluate the robustness of DNS server implementations.
.IP 2. 4
\fBMulti-Server Consistency Comparison (\fB+allcompare\fB)\fR: Users can supply
a comma-separated list of nameservers (e.g.,
\fB@192.0.2.1,198.51.100.1,203.0.113.1:5353\fR). \fBdag\fR queries each server
and can output a matrix comparing response equivalence (\fBMATCH_EXACT\fR,
\fBMATCH_SEMANTIC\fR, or \fB[DIFF]\fR).
.IP 3. 4
\fBWeb Wire-Format Inspection (\fB+ldnsz\fB)\fR: Encodes the raw wire-format
query and response using zlib compression and Base64URL encoding, generating
inspection URLs for ldns.jp <https://ldns.jp/> or multi-server binary diff URLs.
.IP 4. 4
\fBTransports & Protocol Extensions\fR: Support for \fBDNS over TLS (DoT)\fR,
\fBDNS over HTTPS (DoH)\fR, \fBPlain HTTP DNS\fR, \fBHAProxy PROXYv2\fR, \fBEDNS
Client Subnet (ECS)\fR, \fBDNS Cookies\fR, \fBExtended DNS Errors (EDE)\fR,
\fBMultiple QTYPE (RFC 10029)\fR, \fBDynamic Updates (RFC 2136)\fR, and
\fBTransaction Security (TSIG / SIG(0))\fR.
.IP 5. 4
\fBTraffic Replay & Differential Engine (\fB\-\-replay\fB)\fR: Asynchronously
replays recorded DNS queries from PCAP traces, dnstap Frame Streams, or text
query lists against target nameservers, providing order-independent semantic
differential testing across dual servers and automatic protocol preservation
(TCP/UDP). See \fBdag\-replay(1)\fR for full reference.
.IP 6. 4
\fBMemory Arenas\fR: Uses bump-allocated memory arenas (\fBzone_arena_t\fR) for
scratch allocations during query processing.
.SS Default Lookup Behavior
.IP \(bu 2
Unless \fB@server\fR is explicitly provided, \fBdag\fR reads nameserver
addresses from \fB/etc/resolv.conf\fR (on Windows, from the system's DNS server
list). If no server is found, it queries \fB127.0.0.1\fR (on Windows:
\fB1.1.1.1\fR).
.IP \(bu 2
When no domain name is supplied, \fBdag\fR queries the root zone (\fB.\fR) for
\fBNS\fR records. If a domain name is supplied without a type, it defaults to
\fBA\fR (or \fBPTR\fR if \fB\-x\fR is specified). The name, type and class may
be given in any order.
.IP \(bu 2
The query has the RD and AD bits set, like \fBdig\fR.
.IP \(bu 2
Like \fBdig\fR, queries carry an EDNS0 OPT record (version 0, UDP payload size
1232) with a random 8-byte client cookie by default; \fB+noedns\fR and
\fB+nocookie\fR turn them off. Dynamic UPDATE and NOTIFY messages
(\fB\-\-update\-*\fR, \fB\-\-prereq\-*\fR, \fB+opcode=UPDATE\fR,
\fB+opcode=NOTIFY\fR) get no OPT record unless an EDNS option is given
explicitly, like \fBnsupdate\fR.
.IP \(bu 2
In the normal output format, a hex dump of the query (\fBQuery (N bytes):\fR)
and of the response is printed in addition to the dig-style sections. Use
\fB+nohexdump\fR to turn this off; the dumps are not printed with \fB+short\fR
or \fB+yaml\fR (and the response dump not with \fB+nocomments\fR). \fB+noall\fR
turns them off too, so \fB+noall +answer\fR prints only the answer like
\fBdig\fR; \fB+hexdump\fR after \fB+noall\fR brings them back.
.IP \(bu 2
A response that \fBdag\fR receives and then discards (a message shorter than the
12-octet header, a transaction ID, question or opcode mismatch, or a mismatching
client COOKIE; RFC 5452 section 9.1) is dumped as well, right after its warning
line: \fB;; Discarded response (N bytes, UDP from 192.0.2.1#53):\fR followed by
the hex dump of the received octets (\fBTCP\fR over TCP). \fBdig\fR shows only
the warning. The message is still discarded, so the exit code and the retry
behaviour are the same as in \fBdig\fR. This dump follows the response dump: it
is turned off by \fB+nohexdump\fR, \fB+nohexdump\-response\fR,
\fB+nocomments\fR, \fB+noall\fR, \fB+short\fR and \fB+yaml\fR.
.IP \(bu 2
Unlike \fBdig\fR, a truncated UDP response (\fBTC=1\fR) is printed like any
other response before \fB;; Truncated, retrying in TCP mode.\fR and the TCP
retry to the same server; both responses are recorded for the comparison summary
and \fB+ldnsz\fR (see Note on TC (Truncation) Retries). \fB+ignore\fR keeps the
UDP response and does not retry.
.IP \(bu 2
Per-user defaults can be configured via \fB${HOME}/.digrc\fR. This file is read
and its options applied before command-line arguments, unless the \fB\-r\fR
option is supplied.
.IP \(bu 2
Up to 64 queries can be given on one command line (see MULTIPLE QUERIES & BATCH
PROCESSING).
.SH OPTIONS
.SS Query Target & General Options
.TP 8
\fB\-4\fR
Force query transport over IPv4 only.
.TP 8
\fB\-6\fR
Force query transport over IPv6 only.
.TP 8
\fB\-b address[#port]\fR
Set the source IP address and optional source port for the query. \fBaddress\fR
must be a valid interface address on the local host.
.TP 8
\fB\-c class\fR
Set the query class. The default is \fBIN\fR (Internet). Other supported classes
include \fBCH\fR / \fBCHAOS\fR, \fBHS\fR / \fBHESIOD\fR, or numeric
\fBCLASSnn\fR syntax (e.g., \fBCLASS3\fR).
.TP 8
\fB\-f filename\fR
Batch mode. Reads a list of query requests from \fBfilename\fR line by
line. Blank lines and lines beginning with \fB#\fR or \fB;\fR are ignored. Each
line is processed as an independent set of query arguments.
.TP 8
\fB\-h\fR, \fB\-\-help\fR
Print a comprehensive usage summary and exit.
.TP 8
\fB\-\-break\-help\fR
Print the list of all available \fB\-\-break\fR mutation and fuzzing options and
exit.
.TP 8
\fB\-k keyfile\fR
Sign queries using TSIG with credentials read from a BIND-compatible key file,
or with SIG(0) when the file is a BIND \fB.private\fR key (see TSIG TRANSACTION
SECURITY).
.TP 8
\fB\-m\fR
Enable memory usage debugging. Upon completion, \fBdag\fR prints process maximum
resident set size (\fBru_maxrss\fR) via \fBgetrusage(2)\fR. \fI(Not available on
Windows)\fR
.TP 8
\fB\-p port\fR
Send queries to the specified port instead of the default port 53 (or 853 for
DoT, 443 for DoH, 80 for Plain HTTP).
.TP 8
\fB\-q name\fR
Explicitly specify the domain name to query. Useful to distinguish domain names
that overlap with record types or options.
.TP 8
\fB\-r\fR
Do not read options from \fB${HOME}/.digrc\fR.
.TP 8
\fB\-t type\fR
Explicitly specify the resource record type. Supports standard mnemonics
(\fBA\fR, \fBAAAA\fR, \fBNS\fR, \fBSOA\fR, \fBMX\fR, \fBTXT\fR, \fBSRV\fR,
\fBHTTPS\fR, \fBSVCB\fR, \fBDS\fR, \fBDNSKEY\fR, \fBANY\fR, \fBAXFR\fR,
\fBIXFR=serial\fR, etc.) as well as generic \fBTYPEnn\fR syntax (RFC
3597). \fBRESINFO\fR (RFC 9606) and \fBWALLET\fR are printed like \fBTXT\fR
(quoted character-strings), \fBEID\fR and \fBNIMLOC\fR as one hex string, and
\fBNULL\fR, \fBUINFO\fR, \fBUID\fR, \fBGID\fR and \fBUNSPEC\fR in the RFC 3597
\fB\e# <length> <hex>\fR form, as dig 9.20 does.
.TP 8
\fB\-u\fR
Display query elapsed times in microseconds (\[u00B5]s) instead of milliseconds
(ms).
.TP 8
\fB\-v\fR, \fB\-\-version\fR
Display the version string (\fBKariDNS dag v...\fR) and exit.
.TP 8
\fB\-x addr\fR
Simplified reverse DNS lookup for IPv4 and IPv6 addresses. Automatically
converts dotted-decimal IPv4 addresses into \fBin\-addr.arpa\fR and
colon-delimited IPv6 addresses into \fBip6.arpa\fR (nibble format), setting the
query type to \fBPTR\fR and class to \fBIN\fR.
.TP 8
\fB\-y [hmac:]name:secret\fR
Sign queries using TSIG with the provided base64-encoded shared secret. The
algorithm prefix can be \fBhmac\-md5\fR, \fBhmac\-sha1\fR, \fBhmac\-sha224\fR,
\fBhmac\-sha256\fR (default), \fBhmac\-sha384\fR, or \fBhmac\-sha512\fR.
.TP 8
\fB\-\-hex <hex>\fR, \fB\-\-hex=<hex>\fR
Transmit an arbitrary raw DNS wire-format packet provided as a hexadecimal
string (up to 65,535 bytes). Allows direct crafting and replay of custom or
malformed DNS messages.
.SH TRANSPORT & PROTOCOL OPTIONS
.TP 8
\fB+[no]tcp\fR, \fB+[no]vc\fR
Use TCP transport instead of UDP. \fB+vc\fR ("virtual circuit") and
\fB\-\-tcp\fR are synonyms for \fB+tcp\fR. \fB+novc\fR / \fB+notcp\fR force
UDP. \fBAXFR\fR and large \fBIXFR\fR queries automatically elevate to TCP unless
\fB+udp\fR is explicitly forced.
.TP 8
\fB+udp\fR
Force UDP transport.
.TP 8
\fB+tcp\-mss=N\fR
Set the TCP maximum segment size (\fBTCP_MAXSEG\fR) of the query connection to
\fBN\fR bytes. Implies \fB+tcp\fR. Corresponds to the server's \fBtcp\-mss\fR
(see karidns(8)).
.TP 8
\fB+tcp\-window=N\fR
Set the TCP receive and send buffer sizes (\fBSO_RCVBUF\fR / \fBSO_SNDBUF\fR) of
the query connection to \fBN\fR bytes before connecting. Implies \fB+tcp\fR.
.TP 8
\fB+[no]tls\fR
Use \fBDNS over TLS (DoT)\fR (RFC 7858). When enabled, default destination port
switches to 853.
.TP 8
\fB+tls\-ca[=file]\fR
Enable TLS server certificate verification. If a PEM \fBfile\fR is specified,
certificate authorities are loaded from it; otherwise, the system default trust
store is used. \fB+notls\-ca\fR disables certificate validation.
.TP 8
\fB+tls\-certfile=file\fR, \fB+tls\-keyfile=file\fR
Set client certificate chain and private key in PEM format for mutual TLS (mTLS)
authentication. Both options must be provided together.
.TP 8
\fB+tls\-hostname=hostname\fR
Specify the expected Server Name Indication (SNI) and hostname for TLS
certificate verification.
.TP 8
\fB+[no]https[=endpoint]\fR
Use \fBDNS over HTTPS (DoH)\fR (RFC 8484) over TLS. Default port is 443; default
endpoint URI path is \fB/dns\-query\fR. Request method defaults to HTTP POST.
.TP 8
\fB+[no]https\-get[=endpoint]\fR
Use DoH with HTTP GET method (transmitting wire query as Base64URL in
\fB?dns=\fR query parameter).
.TP 8
\fB+[no]https\-post[=endpoint]\fR
Use DoH with HTTP POST method (transmitting wire query in HTTP payload body).
.PP
DoH notes: like \fBdig\fR, the DNS message ID is random (RFC 8484 \[u00A7]4.1
recommends 0 for cache friendliness; \fBdig\fR 9.20 does not use 0
either). \fBdag\fR speaks HTTP/1.1, whereas \fBdig\fR uses HTTP/2 (RFC 8484
\[u00A7]5.2 recommends HTTP/2).
.TP 8
\fB+[no]http\-plain[=endpoint]\fR, \fB+[no]http\-plain\-get[=endpoint]\fR, \fB+[no]http\-plain\-post[=endpoint]\fR
Send DNS queries over unencrypted plain HTTP (RFC 8484 message format). Default
port is 80; default endpoint is \fB/dns\-query\fR; the default method is
POST. \fB+http\fR, \fB+http\-get\fR and \fB+http\-post\fR are aliases.
.TP 8
\fB+[no]proxy[=spec]\fR
Prepend a \fBHAProxy PROXYv2\fR binary header before the DNS packet.
.RS 8
.IP \(bu 2
If \fBspec\fR is omitted (\fB+proxy\fR), sends a PROXYv2 header with \fBLOCAL\fR
command.
.IP \(bu 2
If \fBspec\fR is provided in \fBsrc_addr[#src_port]\-dst_addr[#dst_port]\fR
format (e.g., \fB+proxy=192.0.2.1#1234\-192.0.2.2#53\fR), sends a PROXYv2
\fBPROXY\fR command reflecting the specified connection endpoints.
.IP \(bu 2
On TCP-based transports the header is sent right after the TCP connection is
established, \fBbefore\fR any TLS handshake (DoT/DoH). On UDP it is prepended to
each datagram.
.RE
.TP 8
\fB+[no]proxy\-plain[=spec]\fR
Alias for \fB+proxy\fR, kept for \fBdig\fR/\fBkdig\fR compatibility; behaves
identically.
.TP 8
\fB+[no]keepopen\fR
Keep the TCP, TLS or HTTPS connection open between consecutive queries to the
same server (RFC 7766 connection reuse), e.g. when several queries are given on
the command line or in a batch file.
.TP 8
\fB+[no]dns64prefix\fR
Automatically query \fBipv4only.arpa\fR for \fBAAAA\fR records to discover local
DNS64 prefixes (RFC 7050). Known difference: \fBdig +dns64prefix\fR ignores
\fB+noedns\fR and \fB+qr\fR for its lookups; \fBdag\fR applies them.
.TP 8
\fB+timeout=N\fR, \fB+time=N\fR
Set query network timeout in seconds (default: 5 seconds). \fB+time=N\fR
functions as a synonym/alias.
.TP 8
\fB+tries=N\fR
Set the total number of transmission attempts to \fBN\fR (default: 1 attempt).
.TP 8
\fB+retry=N\fR
Set the number of transmission retries to \fBN\fR (total attempts will be \fBN +
1\fR).
.SH DNS HEADER & QUERY FLAGS
.TP 8
\fB+[no]rec\fR, \fB+[no]recurse\fR, \fB+[no]rdflag\fR
Set or clear the \fBRD (Recursion Desired)\fR header bit. Recursion is enabled
by default. Automatically disabled when \fB+trace\fR or \fB+nssearch\fR is
active.
.TP 8
\fB+[no]adflag\fR
Set or clear the \fBAD (Authenticated Data)\fR bit in the query header.
.TP 8
\fB+[no]cdflag\fR
Set or clear the \fBCD (Checking Disabled)\fR bit in the query header,
requesting the upstream server not to perform DNSSEC validation.
.TP 8
\fB+[no]aaflag\fR, \fB+[no]aaonly\fR
Set or clear the \fBAA (Authoritative Answer)\fR bit in the query header.
.TP 8
\fB+[no]tcflag\fR
Set or clear the \fBTC (Truncation)\fR bit in the query header.
.TP 8
\fB+[no]raflag\fR
Set or clear the \fBRA (Recursion Available)\fR bit in the query header.
.TP 8
\fB+[no]zflag\fR
Set or clear the reserved \fBZ\fR bit in the DNS header.
.TP 8
\fB+opcode=N\fR
Override the DNS header \fBOPCODE\fR. Accepts numeric values (0\[u2013]15) or
standard mnemonic strings (\fBQUERY\fR, \fBIQUERY\fR, \fBSTATUS\fR,
\fBNOTIFY\fR, \fBUPDATE\fR). Example: \fB+opcode=UPDATE\fR or
\fB+opcode=5\fR. \fB+noopcode\fR restores the default (\fBQUERY\fR).
.TP 8
\fB+qid=N\fR
Override the 16-bit DNS Query ID (0\[u2013]65535). If omitted, a
cryptographically secure random ID is generated via \fBarc4random(3)\fR (or
platform CSPRNG / OpenSSL \fBRAND_bytes\fR on non-BSD platforms).
.TP 8
\fB+[no]header\-only\fR
Send a query containing only the 12-byte DNS header with \fBQDCOUNT=0\fR (no
QUESTION section).
.TP 8
\fB+[no]ignore\fR
Ignore truncation (\fBTC=1\fR) in UDP responses instead of automatically
retrying over TCP.
.TP 8
\fB+[no]fail\fR
When querying multiple nameservers or using failover lists, controls whether to
try the next nameserver when receiving a \fBSERVFAIL\fR response.
.TP 8
\fB+[no]trace\fR
Trace the DNS delegation path iteratively starting from the root nameservers
(\fB.\fR). By default (\fB+noglue\fR, matching BIND 9.20+ \fBdig\fR behavior),
\fBdag +trace\fR ignores the \fBADDITIONAL\fR section of referral responses and
resolves the delegated nameserver names (A/AAAA) to follow delegation paths down
to authoritative servers, displaying each intermediate answer. Nameserver names
are resolved through \fB@server\fR when it offers recursion (\fBRA=1\fR in its
root \fBNS\fR response); when \fB@server\fR is authoritative-only (e.g. a root
server address such as \fB@192.0.2.1\fR), they are resolved through the system
resolver (\fB/etc/resolv.conf\fR, port 53) like BIND \fBdig\fR, and names that
cannot be resolved are reported as \fB;; couldn't get address
for '<name>'\fR. Specify \fB+glue\fR to restore the previous behavior of using
in-bailiwick Glue records from the \fBADDITIONAL\fR section (falling back to
resolver lookups only when no glue is present). Honors \fB+tcp\fR and
automatically falls back to TCP when receiving truncated (\fBTC=1\fR)
responses. Like BIND \fBdig\fR, \fB+trace\fR implies \fB+noadditional\fR, so the
\fBADDITIONAL\fR section of each hop is not displayed; give \fB+additional\fR
after \fB+trace\fR to show it again. \fB+trace\fR also implies \fB+dnssec\fR
(every hop is sent with DO=1, so RRSIG, DS and NSEC records are shown) and
\fB+authority\fR (so \fB+noall +answer +trace\fR still shows the referrals), as
\fBdig\fR 9.20 does; options are applied in order, so \fB+trace +nodnssec\fR or
\fB+trace +noauthority\fR turns them off again. The output starts with the same
banner as a normal lookup (\fB; <<>> dag <<>> ...\fR, \fB;; global options:
+cmd\fR). A response whose Authority section has no NS records (an authoritative
NODATA answer, or an RFC 9824 compact denial with RCODE NOERROR) ends the trace
like an answer. Each hop ends with \fB;; Received N bytes from ADDR#PORT(name)
in T ms\fR, where \fBname\fR is the \fB@server\fR text (or the system resolver
address) for the first query and the nameserver name the address belongs to for
the later ones, as in BIND \fBdig\fR. Like BIND \fBdig\fR 9.20, the trace ends
at the authoritative answer: a \fBCNAME\fR (or \fBDNAME\fR) in the final answer
is displayed but not followed; use \fB+trace2\fR to follow CNAME/DNAME chains
across zones.
.TP 8
\fB+[no]trace2[=brief|normal|verbose]\fR
Resolve the name the way a full iterative resolver (BIND \fBnamed\fR, Unbound)
does, \fBwithout relying on any local or system resolver\fR. Unlike
\fB+trace\fR, which asks \fB@server\fR / \fB/etc/resolv.conf\fR for the root NS
set and for nameserver addresses, \fB+trace2\fR primes from built-in root hints
(IANA \fBnamed.root\fR; or \fB@server\fR / \fB+roothints=FILE\fR when given)
with a \fB. NS\fR query (RFC 8109), follows referrals with RD=0, and resolves
nameserver names that come without glue (e.g. \fBexample.jp. NS
ns1.example.net.\fR) itself, starting from the closest zone cut it has already
learned. Referral data outside the queried zone's bailiwick is discarded (RFC
2181 \[u00A7]5.4.1). CNAME and DNAME chains are followed across zones; DS
queries are sent to the parent side of the zone cut. Lame servers (REFUSED,
upward/sideways referrals, non-authoritative empty answers), SERVFAIL and
timeouts make it try the next server; FORMERR/NOTIMP to an EDNS query is retried
without EDNS. CNAME loops, nameserver dependency loops (\fBa.test NS ns.b.alt\fR
/ \fBb.alt NS ns.a.test\fR), recursion depth (8) and the total query budget
(\fB+trace2\-maxqueries\fR, default 200) all end the resolution with \fB;;
resolution failed: <reason>\fR instead of looping. Every hop on the main
delegation path is displayed like \fB+trace\fR (\fB;; Received N bytes from
ADDR#PORT(ns\-name) in T ms\fR). The mode selects how nameserver-name
sub-resolutions are shown: \fBnormal\fR (default) prints one line per
sub-resolution (\fB;; [sub] ns1.example.net \-> 192.0.2.53 (4 queries)\fR),
\fBverbose\fR prints every sub query and response prefixed with \fB;; [sub N]
name/type @addr(ns) for zone\fR, and \fBbrief\fR hides them. A final summary
line reports the status, total/sub-resolution query counts, lame servers,
timeouts and elapsed time. Query IDs are randomized per query. \fB\-p\fR applies
to every hop (useful for test hierarchies); \fB\-4\fR/\fB\-6\fR, \fB+tcp\fR,
\fB+time\fR, \fB+tries\fR, \fB+edns\fR/\fB+bufsize\fR/\fB+dnssec\fR apply to
every query. Glue: by default (and with \fB+glue\fR/\fB+glue=all\fR) any glue
within the bailiwick of the referring zone is used; \fB+glue=indomain\fR or an
explicit \fB+noglue\fR uses only in-domain glue (which a delegation cannot work
without, RFC 9471) and resolves the rest. Glue outside the bailiwick of the
referring zone (e.g. an \fBA\fR record for \fBns.other.example.\fR returned in a
referral from the \fBzone.example.\fR servers) is never trusted, not even with
\fB+glue=all\fR, because those servers are not authoritative for it; it is
reported as \fB;; ignoring out\-of\-bailiwick glue for '<ns>' (not under
<zone>)\fR and the nameserver name is resolved from its own authoritative
servers instead. Implies \fB+noadditional\fR. Cannot be combined with
\fB+trace\fR or \fB+nssearch\fR. \fB+trace2\fR does not validate DNSSEC. Like
\fBdig +trace\fR, the exit status is 0 whenever the trace ran, including when
resolution fails (\fB;; resolution failed: ...\fR / \fB;; trace2: SERVFAIL\fR);
it is 9 only when priming fails (no root server could be reached). When given on
the command line with \fB\-f\fR, \fB+trace2\fR and its options apply to every
line of the batch file.
.TP 8
\fB+[no]qmin\fR, \fB+qmin=a|ns|off\fR
QNAME minimisation (RFC 9156) for \fB+trace2\fR. Off by default. When enabled,
each zone is asked only for the next label below it (\fB+qmin\fR / \fB+qmin=a\fR
probes with type A as RFC 9156 recommends; \fB+qmin=ns\fR probes with type NS as
in RFC 7816) until the delegation for the full name is reached, then the real
question is sent. If a server answers a minimised probe with NXDOMAIN or an
error, \fBdag\fR falls back to the full name (relaxed mode, like Unbound). At
most 10 minimised queries are sent per resolution.
.TP 8
\fB+roothints=FILE\fR
Use root hints from \fBFILE\fR (named.root format: \fB. NS name\fR and \fBname
A/AAAA address\fR lines, TTL and class optional; plain \fBname address\fR lines
are also accepted) instead of the built-in hints for \fB+trace2\fR
priming. \fB@server\fR takes precedence.
.TP 8
\fB+trace2\-maxqueries=N\fR
Abort \fB+trace2\fR after \fBN\fR queries in total (1-100000, default 200),
counting priming and nameserver-name sub-resolutions.
.TP 8
\fB+[no]nssearch\fR
Look up authoritative nameservers for the zone containing the query name and
display the SOA record from each responding nameserver. Honors \fB+tcp\fR and
automatically falls back to TCP when receiving truncated (\fBTC=1\fR)
responses. By default, \fBdag +nssearch\fR resolves nameserver addresses using
the system resolver (matching BIND 9 \fBdig\fR behavior, \fB+noglue\fR). You can
also specify \fB+glue\fR to query nameservers directly using in-bailiwick A/AAAA
records from the \fBADDITIONAL\fR section without consulting
\fB/etc/resolv.conf\fR (useful in isolated or test network environments).
.TP 8
\fB+[no]glue\fR
Control whether in-bailiwick Glue records (A/AAAA) present in the
\fBADDITIONAL\fR section are prioritized over system resolver lookups
(\fB/etc/resolv.conf\fR). For \fB+trace\fR, \fB+noglue\fR is the default
(matching BIND 9.20+ \fBdig\fR, which no longer trusts \fBADDITIONAL\fR section
data while tracing); \fB+glue\fR restores the legacy behavior of following
referral chains via attached glue records. For \fB+nssearch\fR, \fB+noglue\fR is
the default (matching BIND 9 \fBdig\fR), and enabling \fB+glue\fR allows
\fBdag\fR to query authoritative nameservers directly using attached glue
records from NS responses without relying on the system resolver.
.TP 8
\fB+glue=all|indomain\fR
Select which glue records \fB+glue\fR trusts. \fBall\fR is the same as plain
\fB+glue\fR (any A/AAAA in \fBADDITIONAL\fR whose owner matches an NS
target). \fBindomain\fR reproduces the stricter glue checking introduced in BIND
\fBnamed\fR 9.18.41 / 9.20.15 / 9.21.14 (ISC KB: Impact of Stricter Glue
Checking <https://kb.isc.org/docs/strict\-glue>): glue is trusted only when the
NS target (right side) is a subdomain of the NS owner (left side),
e.g. \fBexample.org. NS ns1.example.org.\fR. Sibling or unrelated glue
(e.g. \fBa.example. NS ns1.b.example.\fR) is ignored and reported as \fB;;
ignoring out\-of\-domain glue for '<ns>' (NS of '<zone>')\fR, and those NS names
are resolved instead. This is useful for checking whether a delegation that
relied on out-of-domain glue still resolves on updated BIND resolvers. Applies
to both \fB+trace\fR and \fB+nssearch\fR.
.TP 8
\fB+[no]search\fR, \fB+[no]defname\fR
Enable or disable domain search list processing as defined in
\fB/etc/resolv.conf\fR. Like \fBdig\fR, a name with fewer dots than \fBndots\fR
is tried with each search domain first and then as given, otherwise the other
way round; the next candidate is tried only after \fBNXDOMAIN\fR. Only the final
lookup is printed (use \fB+showsearch\fR to see the others), the server cookie
learned from one candidate is sent with the next, and no comparison table is
printed for the candidates.
.TP 8
\fB+domain=name\fR
Set the search list to contain the single domain \fBname\fR and enable search
processing.
.TP 8
\fB+ndots=N\fR
Set the threshold for the number of dots that must appear in a domain name for
it to be considered absolute before search domain appending takes place.
.TP 8
\fB+[no]idn\fR
Toggle Internationalized Domain Names (IDN) processing for both input and output
simultaneously.
.TP 8
\fB+[no]idnin\fR, \fB+[no]idnout\fR
Independently control IDN conversion for input query domain names (Punycode
encoding via \fBlibidn2\fR) and output response domain names (Unicode decoding).
.SH EDNS0 EXTENSIONS
Queries carry an EDNS0 OPT record and a client cookie by default (like
\fBdig\fR). UPDATE and NOTIFY messages get an OPT record only when one of the
options in this section is given.
.TP 8
\fB+[no]edns[=N]\fR
Send an OPT pseudo-RR (on by default); \fB+edns=N\fR sets the EDNS version to
\fBN\fR (default: 0). \fB+noedns\fR sends no OPT record.
.TP 8
\fB+bufsize=N\fR
Set the advertised EDNS0 UDP buffer size, 0\[u2013]65535 (default: 1232 bytes,
compliant with DNS Flag Day recommendations). Enables EDNS.
.TP 8
\fB+[no]dnssec\fR, \fB+[no]do\fR
Set the \fBDO (DNSSEC OK)\fR bit in the EDNS0 OPT record, requesting DNSSEC RRs
(RRSIG, NSEC, NSEC3, DS) from the authoritative server.
.TP 8
\fB+[no]keepalive\fR
Send the \fBEDNS TCP Keepalive (RFC 7828)\fR option (Option Code 11) in the OPT
pseudo-RR. Like \fBdig\fR 9.20, the option is also sent over UDP, although RFC
7828 \[u00A7]3.2.1 says clients MUST NOT include it in UDP queries (\fBdag\fR
follows \fBdig\fR here). A received value is printed as \fB; TCP\-KEEPALIVE:
10.0 secs\fR (units of 100 ms).
.TP 8
\fB+[no]expire\fR
Send the \fBEDNS EXPIRE (RFC 7314)\fR option (Option Code 9) in the query. A
returned value is printed like \fBdig\fR: \fB; EXPIRE: 1209600 (2 weeks)\fR (in
YAML \fBEXPIRE: 1209600 # 2 weeks\fR).
.TP 8
\fB+[no]cookie[=hex]\fR
Send the \fBDNS Cookie (RFC 7873 / RFC 9018)\fR option. Sent by default with a
random 8-byte client cookie (like \fBdig\fR); \fB+nocookie\fR turns it off. If
\fBhex\fR is supplied, sets the client (8 bytes) or client+server cookie value.
.TP 8
\fB+[no]badcookie\fR
When a cookie was sent and the server returns \fBBADCOOKIE\fR, retry with the
returned Server Cookie (\fB;; BADCOOKIE, retrying.\fR) and, if that is answered
with \fBBADCOOKIE\fR again, over TCP (\fB;; BADCOOKIE, retrying in TCP
mode.\fR), as RFC 7873 \[u00A7]5.3 and \fBdig\fR do. Enabled by default.
.TP 8
\fB+[no]showbadcookie\fR
Print the \fBBADCOOKIE\fR answer (banner, message and statistics, like
\fBdig\fR) before retrying.
.TP 8
\fB+subnet=addr[/prefix]\fR
Send the \fBEDNS Client Subnet (ECS)\fR option (RFC 7871) with the specified
IPv4 or IPv6 network prefix (e.g., \fB+subnet=192.0.2.0/24\fR or
\fB+subnet=2001:db8::/56\fR). Specifying \fB+subnet=0\fR (or \fB0/0\fR,
\fB0.0.0.0/0\fR, \fB::/0\fR) sends an empty source address with prefix length 0
to signal privacy preference. \fB+nosubnet\fR removes the option.
.TP 8
\fB+[no]nsid\fR
Request the \fBName Server Identifier (NSID)\fR option (RFC 5001).
.TP 8
\fB+padding[=N]\fR, \fB+nopadding\fR
Add the EDNS(0) Padding option (RFC 7830) so that the query size becomes a
multiple of the block size \fBN\fR (default: 468, the RFC 8467
recommendation). The expected TSIG record is taken into account when TSIG is
used. Like \fBdig\fR, \fB+qr\fR shows this option as \fB; PADDING:\fR without
its length; a padding option received from the server is shown as \fB; PADDING:
(N bytes)\fR.
.TP 8
\fB+[no]mqtype=TYPE[,TYPE...]\fR
Send the \fBMultiple QTYPE (RFC 10029)\fR EDNS option (Option Code 20),
requesting multiple resource record types (e.g., \fB+mqtype=A,AAAA,HTTPS\fR) in
a single query transaction. Up to 16 types; \fB+nomqtype\fR removes the option.
.TP 8
\fB+ednsopt=code[:hex]\fR
Specify a custom EDNS option code (0\[u2013]65535) and optional payload encoded
in hexadecimal string. Up to 8 \fB+ednsopt\fR parameters can be supplied.
.TP 8
\fB+noednsopt\fR
Clear all configured custom EDNS options.
.TP 8
\fB+ednsflags=N\fR, \fB+[no]ednsflags\fR
Set or reset the raw 16-bit EDNS Z-flags in the OPT record. \fB+ednsflags=N\fR
sets the flag bits to \fBN\fR, while \fB+ednsflags\fR (without \fB=\fR) or
\fB+noednsflags\fR resets the flags to \fB0\fR.
.TP 8
\fB+[no]coflag\fR, \fB+[no]co\fR
Set the \fBCompact Answers OK (CO)\fR flag in the OPT record to signal support
for Compact Denial of Existence.
.TP 8
\fB+[no]ednsnegotiation\fR
Enable EDNS version negotiation fallback if a server returns \fBBADVERS\fR.
.TP 8
\fB+[no]showbadvers\fR
Display diagnostic output when EDNS version negotiation is triggered.
.SH TSIG TRANSACTION SECURITY
\fBdag\fR supports TSIG (RFC 8945) transaction signatures to authenticate
requests (such as AXFR, IXFR, Dynamic Updates, and NOTIFY) against authoritative
servers.
.TP 8
\fB\-y [hmac:]name:secret\fR
Set TSIG key inline. \fBhmac\fR specifies the HMAC algorithm:
.RS 8
.IP \(bu 2
\fBhmac\-md5\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fBhmac\-sha1\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fBhmac\-sha224\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fBhmac\-sha256\fR (default)
.IP \(bu 2
\fBhmac\-sha384\fR
.IP \(bu 2
\fBhmac\-sha512\fR \fBname\fR is the TSIG key identity; \fBsecret\fR is the
base64-encoded secret.
.RE
.TP 8
\fB+tsig=[hmac:]name:secret\fR
Query-option equivalent of \fB\-y\fR.
.TP 8
\fB\-k keyfile\fR
Read TSIG key definition or BIND SIG(0) private key from file. \fBdag\fR
automatically determines the key type:
.RS 8
.IP \(bu 2
\fBTSIG (Shared Secret)\fR: Standard BIND-style key file (e.g., generated by
\fBtsig\-keygen\fR):
.RS 2
.PP
.RS 2
.nf
key "tsig\-key.example.com" {
    algorithm hmac\-sha256;
    secret "6p9y...==";
};
.fi
.RE
.RE
.IP \(bu 2
\fBSIG(0) (Public-Key / DNSSEC Private Key)\fR: BIND \fBdnssec\-keygen\fR
generated \fB.private\fR file (e.g.,
\fBKexample.com.+013+12345.private\fR). When a file starting with
\fBPrivate\-key\-format:\fR is passed, \fBdag\fR automatically switches to
SIG(0) signing, deriving the signer domain name, algorithm, and key tag from the
file and filename without needing extra flags, matching BIND \fBdig\fR behavior.
.RE
.TP 8
\fB+fuzztime[=timestamp]\fR
Manually override the TSIG or SIG(0) signing time (seconds since Unix epoch) to
test clock skew tolerances and replay attack defenses. Default timestamp if
\fB+fuzztime\fR is passed without argument is
\fB1646972129\fR. \fB+nofuzztime\fR restores current system clock.
.SH SIG(0) TRANSACTION SECURITY (RFC 2931 / RFC 3007)
\fBdag\fR supports client-side SIG(0) asymmetric key transaction signatures (RFC
2931 / RFC 3007) for authenticating Dynamic DNS UPDATE and query requests using
public-key cryptography (RSA, ECDSA P-256/P-384, Ed25519,
ML-DSA-44). Server-side verification in KariDNS is out of scope.
.TP 8
\fB+sig0\-pkey=file\fR
Specify the private key file in PKCS#8 / traditional PEM format (\fB.key\fR or
\fB.pem\fR) or BIND DNSSEC private key format (\fB.private\fR). The key
algorithm is auto-detected (supporting RSA, ECDSA P-256, Ed25519, and
ML-DSA-44). Setting this option automatically enables SIG(0) signing.
.TP 8
\fB+sig0\-name=name\fR
Specify the signer's domain name (identity of the KEY RR), e.g.,
\fBupdate\-key.example.com.\fR. Required when using PEM keys; automatically
extracted when using BIND \fB\-k\fR / \fB.private\fR keys.
.TP 8
\fB+sig0\-alg=N\fR
Override the algorithm number (e.g., 8 for RSASHA256, 13 for ECDSAP256SHA256, 15
for ED25519, 18 for MLDSA44). If omitted, \fBdag\fR automatically determines the
algorithm from the loaded private key.
.PP
.RS 4
\fBML-DSA-44 (algorithm 18, \fBMLDSA44\fB)\fR follows
draft-westerbaan-dnssec-mldsa
<https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/draft\-westerbaan\-dnssec\-mldsa/>: the
KEY/DNSKEY public key is the bare 1312-octet FIPS 204 encoding and the signature
the bare 2420-octet pure ML-DSA signature with an empty context string. It needs
\fBdag\fR built against OpenSSL 3.5 or later. A BIND \fB.private\fR file carries
the key in \fBPrivateKey:\fR as either the 32-octet seed or the 2560-octet
expanded private key; a PEM file is read as is (e.g. \fBopenssl genpkey
\-algorithm ML\-DSA\-44\fR). A message with a 2.4 KB signature exceeds the usual
UDP payload sizes, so use \fB+tcp\fR for signed UPDATEs.
.RE
.TP 8
\fB+sig0\-keytag=N\fR
Override the 16-bit key tag (0\[u2013]65535). If omitted, \fBdag\fR
automatically computes the key tag from the public key using RFC 4034 Appendix B
and standard KEY RR wire formatting.
.TP 8
\fB+[no]sig0\fR
Toggle SIG(0) transaction signing. Automatically enabled when \fB+sig0\-pkey\fR
or a BIND \fB.private\fR key via \fB\-k\fR is provided. \fB+nosig0\fR disables
SIG(0) signing.
.PP
.RS 4
\fBNote:\fR TSIG (\fB\-y\fR, \fB+tsig\fR, or \fB\-k <tsig_file>\fR) and SIG(0)
(\fB+sig0\-pkey\fR, \fB+sig0\fR, or \fB\-k <private_file>\fR) are mutually
exclusive per transaction. \fB+fuzztime\fR controls the signature inception and
expiration timestamps for testing.
.RE
.SH DYNAMIC DNS UPDATE (RFC 2136)
\fBdag\fR can formulate and send Dynamic DNS UPDATE requests (\fBOPCODE=5\fR),
supporting record additions, deletions, and prerequisite evaluations. The query
name is used as the zone name. Up to 16 update operations and 16 prerequisites
can be given per message. The header bits between Opcode and RCODE are Z in an
UPDATE message (RFC 2136 \[u00A7]2.2), so RD and AD are sent as 0 unless
\fB+rec\fR or \fB+adflag\fR is given explicitly.
.SS Update Operations
.TP 8
\fB\-\-update\-add <RR>\fR
Add a resource record to the zone. Format: \fB"<name> <ttl> [class] <type>
<rdata>"\fR
.RS 8
.PP
.RS 2
.nf
dag example.com @127.0.0.1 \-k update.key \-\-update\-add "web.example.com 300 IN A 192.0.2.10"
.fi
.RE
.RE
.TP 8
\fB\-\-update\-del <name> [type]\fR
Delete all records for \fB<name>\fR, or delete all records of \fB<type>\fR on
\fB<name>\fR.
.RS 8
.PP
.RS 2
.nf
dag example.com @127.0.0.1 \-k update.key \-\-update\-del "oldhost.example.com A"
.fi
.RE
.RE
.TP 8
\fB\-\-update\-del\-exact <RR>\fR
Delete a specific resource record matching full RDATA.
.RS 8
.PP
.RS 2
.nf
dag example.com @127.0.0.1 \-k update.key \-\-update\-del\-exact "web.example.com 0 IN A 192.0.2.10"
.fi
.RE
.RE
.PP
The RDATA of \fB\-\-update\-add\fR, \fB\-\-update\-del\-exact\fR and
\fB\-\-prereq\-yxrrset\fR may be written in the generic form of RFC 3597
\[u00A7]5, \fB\e# <length> <hex>\fR, for unknown types and for known ones (the
length must equal the number of octets, and for a known type the octets must be
valid RDATA for it). The hex digits may be split into blank-separated tokens; an
operand may have up to 65543 tokens, and a larger one is an error (nothing is
truncated silently). This is how a record that the presentation format cannot
show is sent, for example a zero-length unknown-type RR or a \fBTXT\fR string
with a NUL octet:
.PP
.RS 2
.nf
dag example.com @127.0.0.1 \-k update.key \-\-update\-add 'x.example.com 300 IN TYPE65534 \e# 0'
dag example.com @127.0.0.1 \-k update.key \-\-update\-add 'x.example.com 300 IN TXT \e# 4 03610062'
.fi
.RE
.SS Prerequisites
.TP 8
\fB\-\-prereq\-yxdomain <name>\fR
Prerequisite: Domain \fB<name>\fR must exist (at least one RR of any type).
.TP 8
\fB\-\-prereq\-nxdomain <name>\fR
Prerequisite: Domain \fB<name>\fR must NOT exist.
.TP 8
\fB\-\-prereq\-yxrrset <name> <type> [rdata]\fR
Prerequisite: RRset of \fB<type>\fR on \fB<name>\fR must exist (optionally
matching exact \fB<rdata>\fR).
.TP 8
\fB\-\-prereq\-nxrrset <name> <type>\fR
Prerequisite: RRset of \fB<type>\fR on \fB<name>\fR must NOT exist.
.TP 8
\fB\-\-prereq=<kind:name[:type][:rdata]>\fR
Alternative colon-delimited format for specifying prerequisites (e.g.,
\fB\-\-prereq=nxdomain:host.example.com\fR or
\fB\-\-prereq=yxrrset:host.example.com:A:192.0.2.1\fR).
.SH PROTOCOL ANOMALY GENERATOR & FUZZING (\-\-break)
.RS 4
\fBWarning:\fR \fBIntended for Local Testing & Security Audits Only\fR Do not
execute \fB\-\-break\fR anomaly tests against external or production public DNS
servers without explicit authorization.
.RE
.PP
.RS 4
\fBNote:\fR Only one \fIstructural\fR \fB\-\-break\fR mutation
(e.g. \fBcompression\-loop\fR, \fBcompression\-forward\fR,
\fBlabel\-too\-long\fR, \fBreserved\-length\-bits\fR, \fBoversized\-qname\fR,
\fBtruncated\-question\fR, \fBnotify\-no\-question\fR) can be active per
query. If multiple structural breaks are specified, only the first one is
applied and subsequent ones are ignored with a warning. Transport/header flags
can be combined freely.
.RE
.PP
\fBdag\fR provides built-in packet mutators to test server resilience against
protocol edge cases, malformed wire formats, and parser exploits.
.SS Mutation Kinds (\-\-break <kind>[=<param>])
.TS
lb lbw(22) lbw(22)
l lw(22) lw(22).
\&Mutation Kind	\&Description	T{
Tested Vulnerability / Spec
T}
_
\&\fBcompression\-loop\fR	T{
Generates a self-referencing DNS compression pointer (\fB0xC00C \-> 0xC00C\fR).
T}	T{
Infinite pointer recursion / CPU DoS
T}
\&\fBcompression\-forward\fR	T{
Compression pointer targeting an unread forward offset in the packet.
T}	T{
Out-of-bounds read / illegal pointer
T}
\&\fBlabel\-too\-long[=N]\fR	T{
Sets a domain label length byte to \fBN\fR (\fB63 < N < 192\fR, default: 100).
T}	T{
Buffer overflow (> 63 octet RFC limit)
T}
\&\fBreserved\-length\-bits\fR	T{
Sets label length byte to \fB0x40\fR (unallocated RFC 1035 bit pattern).
T}	T{
Parser crash on unassigned label types
T}
\&\fBoversized\-qname\fR	T{
Builds a QNAME exceeding 255 total octets using chained subdomains.
T}	T{
Domain name buffer overflow
T}
\&\fBqdcount=N\fR	T{
Overrides header \fBQDCOUNT\fR with \fBN\fR (e.g., \fBqdcount=2\fR without
second question).
T}	T{
Question section out-of-bounds reading
T}
\&\fBtruncated\-question\fR	T{
Truncates the wire packet abruptly in the middle of a label or type field.
T}	T{
Premature EOF parsing panic
T}
\&\fBopt\-rdlen=N\fR	T{
Overstates OPT record \fBRDLENGTH\fR (e.g., 500 bytes when actual payload is
small).
T}	T{
EDNS0 RDATA boundary overrun
T}
\&\fBarcount=N\fR	T{
Overrides header \fBARCOUNT\fR to indicate non-existent additional records.
T}	T{
Additional record array indexing errors
T}
\&\fBopcode=N\fR	T{
Overrides the header \fBOPCODE\fR with \fBN\fR (e.g. an unassigned value such as
15).
T}	T{
Unhandled Opcode crash / state machine
T}
\&\fBqr\-bit\fR	T{
Sets the \fBQR\fR bit to 1 on an outgoing query (sending a response as a query).
T}	T{
Server reflection / loop amplification
T}
\&\fBnotify\-no\-question\fR	T{
Sends \fBOPCODE=4\fR (NOTIFY) with \fBQDCOUNT=0\fR.
T}	T{
RFC 1996 missing zone question panic
T}
\&\fBtoo\-short[=N]\fR	T{
Sends only the first \fBN\fR bytes of the message (default: 3).
T}	T{
Short packet header read violation
T}
\&\fBshort\-header[=N]\fR	T{
Alias for \fBtoo\-short[=N]\fR.
T}	\&(same as \fBtoo\-short\fR)
\&\fBtcp\-length\-overclaim[=N]\fR	T{
(\fITCP only\fR) Prefixes a 2-byte TCP length \fBN\fR bytes larger than sent
data (default: \fBN=10\fR).
T}	T{
TCP frame starvation / hanging worker
T}
\&\fBtcp\-zero\-length\fR	T{
(\fITCP only\fR) Sends a 2-byte TCP length prefix of \fB0\fR.
T}	T{
Zero-length packet hang or memory leak
T}
\&\fBtcp\-idle\-hold[=SEC]\fR	T{
(\fITCP only\fR) Sends only the length prefix, holds the connection for up to
\fBSEC\fR seconds (default: \fBSEC=20\fR) and reports when/if the server
disconnects.
T}	T{
Slowloris / connection pool exhaustion
T}
\&\fBupdate\-meta\-type[=N]\fR	T{
(\fIUPDATE only\fR) Injects a meta-type RR of type \fBN\fR (default: 41 = OPT)
into the Update Section.
T}	T{
Meta-RR validation in dynamic updates
T}
.TE
.PP
The TCP-only kinds require \fB+tcp\fR (or \fB\-\-tcp\fR).
.SS Automated Batch Fuzzing
.TP 8
\fB\-\-break all\fR, \fB\-\-test\-all\fR
Sequentially executes the predefined set of built-in anomaly test cases against
the target server (each with a 1-second timeout and a single try), printing the
response status or timeout for each test case.
.TP 8
\fB\-\-hex=<hexstring>\fR
Directly transmits the raw hexadecimal byte stream as a DNS packet without
validation or reconstruction.
.SH MULTI-SERVER QUERY & CONSISTENCY COMPARISON (+allcompare, +ldnsz)
\fBdag\fR allows querying multiple nameservers in parallel or sequentially
within a single invocation.
.SS Multi-Server Target Syntax
Multiple servers are specified via a comma-separated list after \fB@\fR:
.PP
.RS 2
.nf
dag example.com A @192.0.2.1,198.51.100.1,203.0.113.1:5353,[2001:db8::53]
.fi
.RE
.SS Response Equivalence Comparison (+allcompare)
When more than one response to the same question is collected (several servers,
or a UDP answer followed by a TCP retry), \fBdag\fR prints a comparison summary
after the individual answers. Each response is compared with the base response,
the first complete (non-truncated) response to the same question (QNAME, QTYPE,
QCLASS) and, for zone transfers, the same message number. The summary is not
printed for \fB+trace\fR, \fB+trace2\fR and \fB+nssearch\fR, whose responses
answer different questions (BIND \fBdig\fR prints no such table either);
\fB+ldnsz\fR still produces the trace viewer URL for them.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB[BASE]\fR: The reference response.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBMATCH_EXACT\fR: Binary byte-for-byte match (excluding the 16-bit Query ID).
.IP \(bu 2
\fBMATCH_SEMANTIC\fR: Same RCODE, and the same set of resource records in the
answer, authority and additional sections, regardless of record order. By
default TTLs are ignored.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB[DIFF]\fR: Different RCODE or a different record set.
.PP
\fB+allcompare\fR makes the semantic comparison stricter: TTLs are included in
the record hash (column \fBSEM_HASH(+TTL)\fR), so records that differ only in
their TTL are reported as \fB[DIFF]\fR.
.PP
.RS 2
.nf
;; === MULTI\-SERVER COMPARISON SUMMARY ===
SERVER             | PROTO | RCODE   | ANS | AUT | ADD | SEM_HASH   | TIME   | MATCH STATUS
\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+\-\-\-\-\-+\-\-\-\-\-+\-\-\-\-\-+\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-
192.0.2.1          | UDP   | NOERROR |   1 |   0 |   1 | 0x5A3C19E2 |   12ms | [BASE]
198.51.100.1       | UDP   | NOERROR |   1 |   0 |   1 | 0x5A3C19E2 |   18ms | MATCH_SEMANTIC
203.0.113.1#5353   | UDP   | NOERROR |   1 |   0 |   1 | 0x5A3C19E2 |   15ms | MATCH_EXACT
\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+\-\-\-\-\-+\-\-\-\-\-+\-\-\-\-\-+\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-+\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-\-
.fi
.RE
.PP
.RS 4
\fBNote on TC (Truncation) Retries:\fR When a query triggers automatic TCP retry
due to a truncated UDP response (\fBTC=1\fR), \fBdag\fR intentionally displays
both the UDP response and the TCP retry, and records them as separate rows
(\fBUDP\fR, then \fBTCP\fR) in the summary table. This allows users to
explicitly observe and diagnose the transport fallback process (\fBdig\fR prints
only the TCP answer). The truncated response is never the base: the TCP answer
is \fB[BASE]\fR and the UDP row is compared with it. With \fB+ldnsz\fR, the diff
URL carries both messages (UDP first), so the truncated and the complete
response can be compared side by side.
.RE
.SS LDNSZ Web Inspection URLs (+ldnsz)
When \fB+ldnsz\fR is supplied:
.IP \(bu 2
\fBSingle Server\fR: Generates a web inspector URL on
\fBhttps://ldns.jp/?dnsz=<payload>\fR, allowing detailed GUI analysis of
wire-format packets.
.IP \(bu 2
\fBMultiple Servers\fR: Generates a diff URL on
\fBhttps://ldns.jp/diff/#c=<payload1>,<payload2>\fR, allowing visual
side-by-side binary comparison.
.IP \(bu 2
\fB\fB+trace\fB\fR: Generates a trace viewer URL on
\fBhttps://ldns.jp/trace/#c=<payload1>,<payload2>,...\fR (one payload per
delegation hop, same encoding as the diff URL), allowing the delegation path to
be inspected step by step.
.SH DISPLAY & FORMATTING OPTIONS
.TP 8
\fB+[no]short\fR
Provide a terse, machine-readable answer containing only the RDATA of the ANSWER
section.
.TP 8
\fB+[no]multiline\fR, \fB+[no]multi\fR
Print records (such as SOA, DNSKEY, RRSIG, and HTTPS) in human-readable
multi-line format with field descriptions and structured comments. Short form
(\fB+[no]multi\fR) is also supported.
.TP 8
\fB+[no]yaml\fR
Output the parsed DNS messages in the YAML format of \fBdig +yaml\fR. The
display options apply as in \fBdig\fR: \fB+noall\fR, \fB+[no]question\fR,
\fB+[no]answer\fR, ... select the sections, \fB+nocomments\fR drops
\fBOPT_PSEUDOSECTION\fR, \fB+qr\fR prints the query as \fBRECURSIVE_QUERY\fR (or
\fBAUTH_QUERY\fR with \fB+norec\fR), and \fBquery_time\fR/\fBresponse_time\fR
carry the real send and receive times (milliseconds). A TSIG or SIG(0) record is
shown as \fBTSIG_PSEUDOSECTION\fR / \fBSIG0_PSEUDOSECTION\fR.
.TP 8
\fB+[no]ttlunits\fR
Display TTL values using human-friendly time unit suffixes (\fBs\fR, \fBm\fR,
\fBh\fR, \fBd\fR, \fBw\fR). Implies \fB+ttlid\fR.
.TP 8
\fB+[no]class\fR
Toggle display of the CLASS field in record listings.
.TP 8
\fB+[no]ttlid\fR
Toggle display of the TTL field in record listings.
.TP 8
\fB+[no]unknownformat\fR
Format all record RDATA using RFC 3597 unknown record presentation format
(\fB\e# <length> <hex>\fR).
.TP 8
\fB+[no]crypto\fR
Toggle display of raw cryptographic key data in DNSKEY, DS, and RRSIG
records. When disabled, keys are displayed as \fB[ key id = ... ]\fR or
\fB[omitted]\fR.
.TP 8
\fB+[no]rrcomments\fR
Display explanatory inline comments for DNSSEC records (e.g., Key Tag, algorithm
name). As in \fBdig\fR, \fB+multiline\fR turns them on unless
\fB+norrcomments\fR is given: \fB) ; KSK; alg = ECDSAP256SHA256 ; key id =
23534\fR for DNSKEY/CDNSKEY, \fB); alg = ... ; key id = N\fR for KEY/RKEY, and
the SOA field comments.
.TP 8
\fB+[no]comments\fR
Toggle display of comment banners (;; ->>HEADER<<-, opcode, status, flags).
.TP 8
\fB+[no]cmd\fR
Toggle printing of the initial command-line version banner.
.TP 8
\fB+[no]stats\fR
Toggle printing of query statistics (query time, server IP, timestamp, message
size).
.TP 8
\fB+[no]question\fR, \fB+[no]answer\fR, \fB+[no]authority\fR, \fB+[no]additional\fR
Individually toggle display of the respective DNS packet sections.
.TP 8
\fB+[no]all\fR
Turn all display flags (sections, comments, statistics, command banner and the
hex dumps) on (\fB+all\fR) or off (\fB+noall\fR).
.TP 8
\fB+[no]qr\fR
Print the outgoing query before transmitting. As in \fBdig\fR, the display
options apply to it, a TSIG record is shown as \fB;; TSIG PSEUDOSECTION:\fR,
\fB;; QUERY SIZE\fR belongs to \fB+stats\fR, and zone transfer queries are shown
as plain records.
.TP 8
\fB+[no]identify\fR
When \fB+short\fR is enabled, display the responding server IP and port
alongside the answer.
.TP 8
\fB+[no]idn\fR
Enable or disable Internationalized Domain Name (IDN) Punycode conversion.
.TP 8
\fB+[no]onesoa\fR
Print only the initial SOA record during AXFR transfers instead of both starting
and ending SOAs.
.TP 8
\fB+[no]expandaaaa\fR
Display IPv6 AAAA record addresses in fully expanded 8-group notation
(e.g. \fB2001:0db8:0000:0000:...\fR) instead of compressed notation.
.TP 8
\fB+[no]split=N\fR
Split long base64 and hex strings into chunks of \fBN\fR characters, rounded up
to a multiple of 4 (default: 56; 44 in multiline mode). \fB+nosplit\fR or
\fB+split=0\fR disables splitting.
.TP 8
\fB+[no]besteffort\fR
Accepted for \fBdig\fR compatibility. Like \fBdig\fR 9.20, \fBdag\fR prints
malformed responses with or without it (see Malformed Responses).
.TP 8
\fB+[no]showsearch\fR
Print every lookup of search list processing, including the \fBNXDOMAIN\fR
answers that make \fBdag\fR try the next candidate (implies \fB+search\fR).
.TP 8
\fB+[no]hexdump\fR
Show (default) or suppress the hex dumps of both the outgoing query and the
incoming response.
.TP 8
\fB+[no]hexdump\-query\fR
Show or suppress the hex dump of the outgoing query only.
.TP 8
\fB+[no]hexdump\-response\fR
Show or suppress the hex dump of the incoming response only. It also controls
the dump of a discarded response (see "Default Lookup Behavior").
.SS dig-compatible message presentation
.IP \(bu 2
Records and question lines are laid out like \fBdig\fR 9.20: each field starts
at a fixed column and is reached with tabs (tab width 8) and spaces, or a single
space when the previous field already passed the column (BIND
\fBmasterdump.c\fR). The columns of TTL, class, type and RDATA are 24/32/40/48,
24/24/32/40 with \fB+nottlid\fR or \fB+noclass\fR, and 24/24/24/32 with
\fB+multiline\fR or with both \fB+nottlid\fR and \fB+noclass\fR; the question
line has no TTL and always shows the class. Continuation lines of
\fB+multiline\fR RDATA are indented to the RDATA column (four tabs). Scripts
that split the output should split on white space, not on single spaces.
.IP \(bu 2
Type 128 (RFC 9824 \fBNXNAME\fR) is printed as \fBTYPE128\fR, as \fBdig\fR 9.20
does (also in NSEC type bit maps); \fBNXNAME\fR is still accepted as input.
.IP \(bu 2
Names are printed in presentation format like \fBdig\fR: besides \fB.\fR and
\fB\e\fR, the characters \fB"\fR \fB(\fR \fB)\fR \fB;\fR \fB@\fR \fB$\fR are
escaped (\fBc\e(p.example.\fR), and octets outside 0x21-0x7E as \fB\eDDD\fR, in
every format (normal, \fB+short\fR, \fB+yaml\fR).
.IP \(bu 2
EDNS options appear in the order received: \fBNSID: 6b 61 ("ka")\fR,
\fBCLIENT\-SUBNET: 192.0.2.0/24/0\fR, \fBCOOKIE: ... (good|bad|echoed)\fR (no
status when no cookie was sent), \fBEXPIRE: N (duration)\fR, \fBTCP\-KEEPALIVE:
10.0 secs\fR, \fBPADDING: (N bytes)\fR, \fBEDE: 18 (Prohibited): (text)\fR
(codes above 24 by number only), and unknown options as \fBOPT=65001: de ad
("..")\fR. \fBdag\fR sends its own options in \fBdig\fR's order (NSID,
CLIENT-SUBNET, COOKIE, EXPIRE, TCP-KEEPALIVE, \fB+ednsopt\fR, PADDING).
.IP \(bu 2
RCODE names follow \fBdig\fR: the extended RCODE 16 is \fBBADVERS\fR, 11-15 are
\fBRESERVEDn\fR, other unnamed values \fB?N\fR; the TSIG Error field uses
\fBBADSIG\fR, \fBBADKEY\fR, \fBBADTIME\fR, ... An unassigned opcode is
\fBRESERVEDn\fR.
.IP \(bu 2
Signature verification failures read like \fBdig\fR: \fB;; Couldn't verify
signature: tsig indicates error\fR (the server set the TSIG Error), \fBtsig
verify failure\fR (MAC or key mismatch), \fBclocks are unsynchronized\fR,
\fBexpected a TSIG or SIG(0)\fR.
.IP \(bu 2
Zone transfers are printed as records only; an error answer prints its records
(for example the TSIG) and \fB; Transfer failed.\fR without
statistics. AXFR/IXFR queries are sent with RD=0, as \fBdig\fR does.
.IP \(bu 2
An empty line precedes the command banner, the first section after the OPT
pseudosection has no empty line before it, and an empty line follows the
statistics, as in \fBdig\fR. \fB;; WHEN:\fR uses a zero-padded day (\fBTue Oct
06 ...\fR).
.SS Malformed Responses
\fBdag\fR parses responses with the same outcome as \fBdig\fR 9.20 (BIND
\fBdns_message_parse()\fR), whether or not \fB+besteffort\fR is given:
.IP \(bu 2
An answer shorter than the 12-octet header, with the wrong ID or opcode, or
whose question differs from the one sent is reported (\fB;; Warning: short (<
header size) message received\fR, \fB;; Warning: ID mismatch: expected ID N, got
M\fR, \fB;; Warning: Opcode mismatch: expected QUERY, got STATUS\fR, \fB;; ;;
Question section mismatch: got NAME/TYPE/CLASS\fR) and ignored; \fBdag\fR keeps
waiting for the right answer until the timeout (RFC 5452 \[u00A7]9.1). Each
timed-out attempt prints \fB;; communications error to ADDR#PORT: timed
out\fR. An answer without the QR bit is accepted with \fB;; Warning: query
response not set\fR.
.IP \(bu 2
Over TCP \fBdag\fR does not wait for another answer, like \fBdig\fR: an answer
shorter than the header or with the wrong ID ends the lookup with \fB;; ERROR:
short (< header size) message\fR or \fB;; ERROR: ID mismatch: expected ID N, got
M\fR, and a question mismatch ends it after the warning; nothing else is printed
and the exit code is 0. An answer with another opcode is reported and \fBdag\fR
reads the next message on the same connection; if the server closes it, \fB;;
communications error to ADDR#PORT: end of file\fR counts as a failed attempt.
.IP \(bu 2
With \fB+short\fR the same parse warnings and "Got bad packet" dumps are
printed, followed by the records that parsed. With \fB+yaml\fR no \fB;;\fR line
is printed: a message that cannot be parsed prints nothing, a malformed message
shows only the records (and the OPT pseudo-RR) that parsed, and a lookup that
gets no usable answer prints a \fB\- type: DIG_ERROR\fR document. The YAML
\fBtype\fR is \fBRECURSIVE_RESPONSE\fR only when both RD and RA are set, a set Z
bit is shown as \fBMBZ: 0x4\fR, and an unnamed RCODE as its number.
.IP \(bu 2
A name whose compression pointer does not point backwards (loops, forward
pointers; RFC 1035 \[u00A7]4.1.4), a name that needs more compression pointers
than BIND 9.20.30 follows (CVE-2026-19630: more than 2 in a row that point at
pointers, more than 128 for one name, more than 4 per octet of the message over
all its names), a label of type 01 or 10, or a name longer than 255 octets makes
the message unusable: \fBdag\fR prints \fB;; Got bad packet: bad compression
pointer\fR (\fBbad label type\fR, \fBname too long\fR), the size and a dump in
\fBdig\fR's format, and nothing else.
.IP \(bu 2
Running out of data, or RDATA that does not fit its type (A/AAAA length, names
in NS/CNAME/MX/SOA/SRV/..., TXT strings, SVCB parameters, OPT options), ends the
parse at that record: \fB;; Warning: Message parser reports malformed message
packet.\fR, the records before it, and \fB;; WARNING: Message has N extra bytes
at end\fR counted from the start of the bad RDATA.
.IP \(bu 2
A second OPT record, an OPT outside the ADDITIONAL section, more than one
question, or a record whose class differs from the question's is reported as
malformed, and the message is still shown (stray OPT records as \fB. 0 CLASS4096
OPT\fR). A response without a question prints \fB;; missing question section\fR.
.IP \(bu 2
Data after the last record only produces the "extra bytes" warning.
.SH MULTIPLE QUERIES & BATCH PROCESSING
\fBdag\fR allows specifying multiple query tuples on a single command line:
.PP
.RS 2
.nf
dag +qr example.com A @192.0.2.53 +subnet=192.0.2.0/24 \-x 192.0.2.1 @198.51.100.53 +noqr
.fi
.RE
.PP
In this mode:
.IP \(bu 2
Options preceding the first domain name tuple act as \fBglobal defaults\fR.
.IP \(bu 2
Query-specific options override the global options for that particular query.
.IP \(bu 2
Options like \fB+cmd\fR and \fB+short\fR maintain global effect across all
queries.
.IP \(bu 2
As in \fBdig\fR, the \fB; <<>> dag <<>>\fR banner is printed once, for the first
query.
.PP
\fB\-f <file>\fR reads queries from a file, one line at a time, exactly as
\fBdig\fR 9.20 does: lines starting with \fB#\fR or \fB;\fR and empty lines are
skipped only before the first query; every later line is parsed as a query line
(a later \fB; note\fR line queries \fB;.\fR and \fBnote.\fR, a later empty line
queries \fB. NS\fR, the default query). A line may hold several queries (name /
type / class tuples, as on the command line). Every line starts from all the
options given on the command line (transport, display, EDNS, TSIG,
\fB@server\fR, \fB\-p\fR, ...) and the options on the line override them, except
\fB+[no]tcp\fR and \fB+[no]vc\fR, which \fBdig\fR 9.20 ignores on batch lines
(the transport is the one given on the command line); the lines run in one
process, so \fB+keepopen\fR keeps the TCP/TLS/HTTPS connection open across
lines. The first query prints the full banner, later lines only the \fB; <<>>
dag <<>> ...\fR line. The exit status is that of the last line that failed
(e.g. 9 when its servers could not be reached), 0 when all lines got an answer.
.PP
.RS 2
.nf
dag @192.0.2.53 +tcp +keepopen +nohexdump \-f queries.txt
.fi
.RE
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP 8
\fB0\fR
Successful transaction. A valid DNS response was received from the server
(including \fBNXDOMAIN\fR, \fBREFUSED\fR, or other standard DNS error RCODEs).
.TP 8
\fB1\fR
Usage error, invalid command-line options, or DNS query formulation failure.
.TP 8
\fB8\fR
Could not open batch file specified via \fB\-f\fR.
.TP 8
\fB9\fR
Network error or timeout. No reply was received from any target nameserver.
.TP 8
\fB10\fR
Internal execution error or out of memory.
.SH FILES
.TP 8
\fB/etc/resolv.conf\fR
Default system nameserver configuration and search domains.
.TP 8
\fB${HOME}/.digrc\fR
User default options applied automatically on every invocation unless \fB\-r\fR
is provided.
.SH EXAMPLES
.SS Basic Lookups
.RS 2
.nf
# Query A record using system resolver
dag example.com A
\&
# Query specific nameserver on custom port
dag example.com AAAA @127.0.0.1 \-p 5353
\&
# Reverse DNS lookup
dag \-x 192.0.2.53 @127.0.0.1
.fi
.RE
.SS DNSSEC & Extended Protocol Testing
.RS 2
.nf
# Query with DO bit, NSID, and EDNS Client Subnet
dag example.com A @127.0.0.1 +dnssec +nsid +subnet=203.0.113.0/24
\&
# Output structured YAML with microsecond resolution
dag example.com ANY @127.0.0.1 +yaml \-u
\&
# Trace delegation hierarchy from root
dag example.com A +trace
\&
# Resolve like a full resolver, without any local resolver
dag example.jp A +trace2
\&
# Same, showing every query made to resolve glueless nameservers, with QNAME minimisation
dag example.jp A +trace2=verbose +qmin
.fi
.RE
.SS Multi-Server Comparison & LDNSZ Integration
.RS 2
.nf
# Compare answers across three servers (TTLs included)
dag example.com A @192.0.2.1,198.51.100.1,203.0.113.1 +allcompare
\&
# Generate visual online diff URL
dag example.com A @192.0.2.1,198.51.100.1 +ldnsz
.fi
.RE
.SS Modern Transports (DoT / DoH / PROXYv2)
.RS 2
.nf
# Query via DNS over TLS (DoT)
dag example.com A @127.0.0.1 +tls +tls\-ca=/etc/ssl/cert.pem
\&
# Query via DNS over HTTPS (DoH) using GET
dag example.com A @doh.example.net +https\-get
\&
# Query via HAProxy PROXYv2 encapsulation
dag example.com A @127.0.0.1 +proxy=192.0.2.10#45000\-192.0.2.1#53
.fi
.RE
.SS Zone Transfers & Updates
.RS 2
.nf
# Full zone transfer over TCP with TSIG
dag example.com AXFR @127.0.0.1 \-k /etc/rndc.key
\&
# Incremental zone transfer from serial 2026082301
dag example.com IXFR=2026082301 @127.0.0.1
\&
# Dynamic DNS Update (add record with prerequisite)
dag example.com @127.0.0.1 \-k /etc/rndc.key \e
    \-\-prereq\-nxdomain newhost.example.com \e
    \-\-update\-add "newhost.example.com 300 IN A 192.0.2.99"
\&
# Dynamic DNS Update with SIG(0) transaction signature (RFC 3007 / RFC 2931)
dag example.com @127.0.0.1 +sig0\-pkey=key.pem +sig0\-name=update\-key.example.com. \e
    \-\-update\-add "newhost.example.com 300 IN A 192.0.2.99"
.fi
.RE
.SS Protocol Fuzzing & Anomaly Testing
.RS 2
.nf
# Test server compression loop handling
dag example.com A @127.0.0.1 \-\-break compression\-loop
\&
# Test oversized label handling
dag example.com A @127.0.0.1 \-\-break label\-too\-long=120
\&
# Run complete automated anomaly fuzzing suite
dag example.com A @127.0.0.1 \-\-test\-all
.fi
.RE
